<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Earldom of Lade</h1><p class="st-meta">880 CE – 1030 CE (approx.) · capital Lade</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Dynasty of earls based near Trondheim who rivalled the descendants of Harald Fairhair and ruled Norway for long periods around 1000.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The earls took their name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lade-trondheim">Lade</a>, a farm near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a> in Trøndelag. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakon-grjotgardsson">Håkon Grjotgardsson</a>, whose family came from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/halogaland">Hålogaland</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> in the late 9th century and was made earl of the Trøndelag region, founding a powerful line.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The jarls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lade-trondheim">Lade</a> drew strength from the rich farming districts and assemblies of Trøndelag and from the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakon-sigurdsson">Håkon Sigurdsson</a>, known as Haakon Jarl, ruled most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from about 975 to 995 under the overlordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and was a champion of the pagan cults. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-svolder">Battle of Svolder</a> in 1000, his sons Eric and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-haakonsson">Sweyn</a> governed Norway for the kings of Denmark and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf Haraldsson</a> drove out the earls in 1015. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakon-eiriksson">Håkon Eiriksson</a> as his regent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1028, but Håkon drowned around 1030 and the line ended. Trøndelag passed fully to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a> (Nidaros) became its religious centre.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/earldom-of-lade.jpg" alt="Earldom of Lade" width="320" height="641"><figcaption>Image: Marit Sofie Wiggen · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ladejarlen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>880 CE – 1030 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lade</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Håkon Grjotgardsson, Haakon Sigurdsson, Eric Håkonsson</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lade jarls, Hlaðir, Trøndelag</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=955&amp;focus=earldom-of-lade" aria-label="Earldom of Lade on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4891 85 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Earldom of Lade lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5211 341L5225 425M5211 341L5274 535"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 425h0M5274 535h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5211 341h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5211 341h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lade. <a href="/atlas?year=955&amp;focus=earldom-of-lade">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

